  "story": {
    "headline": "A New Study Points to Two Origins of Life on Earth by Tracing Early Chemical Reactions in Single-Celled Organisms",
    "summary": "The work challenges the assumption that all life descended from one free-living cell. Instead, it suggests that two microbial lineages, the bacteria and archaea, evolved independently from a primordial, nonliving ancestor",
    "publisher": "Rally News",
    "url": "https://rally.news/categories/world/?article=A%2BNew%2BStudy%2BPoints%2Bto%2BTwo%2BOrigins%2Bof%2BLife%2Bon%2BEarth%2Bby%2BTracing%2BEarly%2BChemical%2BReactions%2Bin%2BSingle-Celled%2BOrganisms"
  },
  "researchBrief": "The study published in Science Advances suggests that life on Earth may have originated twice, with bacteria and archaea evolving independently from a primordial, nonliving ancestor. This challenges the long-held assumption that all life descended from one free-living cell. The research team retraced the steps of early metabolic reactions and found that the last universal common ancestor (LUCA) relied on both enzymes and metals in the environment to conduct metabolism. Over time, these enzymes evolved to replace the metals, leading to the independent emergence of free-living bacteria and archaea. The study was corroborated by multiple sources, including Smithsonian Magazine, Phys.org, and ScienceAlert, which all highlighted the significance of the findings. The key numbers and specific enzymes involved were not mentioned in the sources, but the overall conclusion of two independent origins of life was consistently reported.",
  "sources": [
    "https://www.newscientist.com/article/1848230-the-second-coming-did-life-evolve-on-earth-more-than-once/",
